A||N||11||turbo86se||can A/C be added?||||5||
Z||000000||turbo86se||02-15-2000||05:09 PM||||<BR>Hello all, I just found a nice 87 coupe today in a local neighborhood. It's in good condition, great interior, needs body work, 60k, 5-spd, burgandy paint, new clutch, but doesn't have A/C! Down in the south, A/C is a necessity! I don't think my g'friend will enjoy riding around in this one! I do have several parts cars, including a 87 with A/C. Can everything be swapped over? TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||02-15-2000||05:15 PM||||I don't see why not. It would be an awful lot of work though. You would need a different alternator, the condensor would have to be mounted up front, the current system you own would have to be professionally drained, tons of a/c lines running back and forth, wiring...<BR>If your up to it, I say go for it, but it sounds like a major project.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||02-15-2000||06:02 PM||||Might be easier to pull good pieces off of the new car and add them to the parts car depending on the conditions of each car respectively.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000003||theogre||02-15-2000||06:53 PM||||This is major work. You will also need to replace every seal in the system and the orifice tube.<P>You'll need to replace a major portion of what's under the dash.<P>you'll need to do a bunch of wiring as well.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||02-15-2000||07:11 PM||||Another thing to consider is the condition of the donor AC. Did it work? Does it still have at least some pressure? It would be a real pain to do all that work then find out you have to replace the compressor and/or other parts you thought were good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>As mentioned above the stuff should fit and it will be A LOT of work. Having the complete donor car sitting there is the only way I would even consider doing it. <P>Personally, I don't think I would do it unless the car was an Indy (would probably have AC anyway)... or I got it for practically nothing... or I was looking for something to kill time... etc...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||02-15-2000||07:37 PM||||"Orifice tube"???  I probably shouldn't go there, huh?||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||02-15-2000||10:45 PM||||I have installed a/c in non a/c cars and trucks before. I have never had the chance or reason to do a fiero. I would like to. If you would like I will get you the parts at my cost and you do the work. I will also do the research. People that know me, know that my parts prices a cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000007||turbo86se||02-16-2000||12:18 AM||||Thanks a lot guys! I really apprecieate the help! This car is the last one of three I've considered buying. First is an 86GT that needs a clutch, then a low-mileage mint 88 coupe that needs engine work, and the non A/C 87 coupe! But, I need to sell my T-Top 88 coupe first. TOM, please email me about the parts!  TIA||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||02-17-2000||09:24 AM||||For what that swap is gonna cost, you might be able to find another complete Fiero with working air...just drive the "airless" one in the cool months.<P>There you go...reason to buy another Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000009||TOM||02-17-2000||10:25 AM||||What cost tou mean like Between $200 and $300. I have never seen a decent fiero for that much||||reg||1
Z||000010||deceler8||02-17-2000||11:43 AM||||I guess I just assumed that you'd need a new drier, probably a new compressor, and once you dove in, you'd need a bunch of other stuff that would drive the price for materials and labor past $500.<P>And I'm curious too as I have non-working air, but all the parts are still there. <P>Just seems like a lot of work.||||reg||1
Z||000011||TOM||02-17-2000||09:35 PM||||$500? Wanna but some parts?||||reg||1
